WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:04.600
It should be alright! There it goes! Aww,
actually that’s quite

00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:06.210
spectacular at the end.

00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:10.550
And in my second-year, I think I love boron
far too much I think,

00:00:10.550 --> 00:00:12.750
‘cause I talk about it too much.

00:00:12.750 --> 00:00:14.599
You love boron?

00:00:14.599 --> 00:00:17.000
I love boron! No, I used to do lots of chemistry
with boron, I

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:20.150
kind of moved away a little bit from boron
now but I really

00:00:20.150 --> 00:00:21.150
like it.

00:00:21.150 --> 00:00:25.610
Boron is an interesting element. It is one
of the few that I

00:00:25.610 --> 00:00:28.260
have actually made myself when I was at school.

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:32.020
Boron sits on top of the group 13. Its atomic
number is 5, so

00:00:32.020 --> 00:00:35.300
it is a metalloid, so elemental boron is a
metalloid which

00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:37.630
basically means that it has some of the properties
of a non

00:00:37.630 --> 00:00:39.820
metal and some of the properties of a metal.

00:00:39.820 --> 00:00:45.910
I mixed an oxide of boron with magnesium,
heated it up and

00:00:45.910 --> 00:00:51.399
then, it is a really dramatic reaction when
you add water at

00:00:51.399 --> 00:00:56.489
the end to try and destroy the magnesium boride
that has

00:00:56.489 --> 00:01:00.399
formed because you get puffs of gas coming
off which burst

00:01:00.399 --> 00:01:06.020
into flames rather like little anti-aircraft
guns. But boron itself

00:01:06.020 --> 00:01:08.800
was rather disappointing; it’s a sort of
brown powder.

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:11.490
I mean there is a more of a powdery brown
boron, there are

00:01:11.490 --> 00:01:14.750
several crystalline sort of types of alatropes
of boron. There is

00:01:14.750 --> 00:01:17.060
one actually that is really, really hard,
so the black crystalline

00:01:17.060 --> 00:01:20.840
boron is actually very, very hard. It’s
just below diamond on

00:01:20.840 --> 00:01:25.549
Mohs scale of hardness, so I believe. And
actually in America,

00:01:25.549 --> 00:01:29.060
there is a town called Boron. It’s in California,
it’s got a

00:01:29.060 --> 00:01:32.119
population of around 2000 which actually was
set up around

00:01:32.119 --> 00:01:34.270
the world’s biggest borax mine which is
in California.

00:01:34.270 --> 00:01:40.930
Well it used to be transported on trains of
mules and the

00:01:40.930 --> 00:01:49.590
company that produced it used to have as their
trademark a

00:01:49.590 --> 00:01:52.450
train of 20 mules sort of going along.

00:01:52.450 --> 00:01:56.450
Of great interest to us chemists, well over
the last sort of 50,

00:01:56.450 --> 00:01:59.740
maybe sort of 60-70 years, have been the boron
hydrides or

00:01:59.740 --> 00:02:04.880
borades. So boron hydrides are basically clusters
of borons

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:08.860
with hydrogens around them. And one of particular
use and

00:02:08.860 --> 00:02:12.000
interest in the 50s was one called pentaborane,
which

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:15.690
basically: penta, five, it’s got five borons
in it. And

00:02:15.690 --> 00:02:19.720
Pentaborate was investigated during the cold
war or in the

00:02:19.720 --> 00:02:25.140
early part of the cold war as being a rocket
fuel. It actually

00:02:25.140 --> 00:02:28.180
burns with a green flame. They actually called
it the ‘green

00:02:28.180 --> 00:02:31.500
dragon’ because it burns with a very, very
hot green flame

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:34.570
and also because it is pretty toxic, which
I can actually show

00:02:34.570 --> 00:02:41.170
you if I … It is probably not going to be
spectacular.

00:02:41.170 --> 00:02:46.921
And boron complexes or borax compounds are
used in a

00:02:46.921 --> 00:02:49.611
variety of things. It is not a particularly
expensive material. It

00:02:49.611 --> 00:02:53.250
is used in washing powder, particularly perborate
which when

00:02:53.250 --> 00:02:58.730
you heat it up in the washing machine when
the water gets to

00:02:58.730 --> 00:03:02.580
60 degrees centigrade it turns into hydrogen
peroxide which

00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:07.150
can then bleach the clothes and this is why
the famous

00:03:07.150 --> 00:03:12.580
washing powder is called Persil, because the
‘per’ stands for

00:03:12.580 --> 00:03:16.560
perborate and the ‘sil’ stands for silicate
so it is really a

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:18.959
mixture of perborate and silicate.

00:03:18.959 --> 00:03:24.720
Ok so this isn’t pentaborane because pentaborane
spontaneously combusts in the air. This is

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:29.790
just benzene
boronic acid. It’s an organic compound with

00:03:29.790 --> 00:03:32.769
some boron in it.
So hopefully we can see, we should be able

00:03:32.769 --> 00:03:35.260
to see, if I can
get some at the end of my spatula, that it

00:03:35.260 --> 00:03:38.129
should be able to
burn with some sort of green flame. Hey, there

00:03:38.129 --> 00:03:42.190
it is. You can
also see a bit of yellow which is the organic

00:03:42.190 --> 00:03:46.290
bit going as well.
It should be alright! There it goes! Aww,

00:03:46.290 --> 00:03:51.960
actually that was
quite spectacular at the end. Ok.

